[NUM] + [EXACT COMPUTATION] Convergent denominators in range are {1,2,5,12}. Positive strata include k=4,6,8,9 with {kα}-distance to nearest integer 0.34, 0.49, 0.32, 0.26 — large. The best-approximated k=12 (dist 0.0196) is unremarkable (7/37 positive), k=2 (a convergent!) is the *least* positive stratum anywhere (2/38, 5%), and k=1 is structurally zero on all 110 rows ([PROOF]: n₁=1 single-delta mass, shift 3≢0 mod N). Meanwhile non-convergent k=11,13 lead (34%, 48% positive rate). Census table is finite — not a theorem — but the direction is anti-correlation if anything.

[DIAGNOSTIC] The two mechanisms are arithmetically different objects: Lemma L is 2-adic divisibility (2^A(W) | index gaps) forcing gaps in an *infinite orbit*; L_lift is signed sampling of a *spatial* autocorrelation at the modular shift 3^k mod 2^(s+1). Worse, Lemma L lives at scales q≥53, beyond every stratum of the m≤24 grid — the overlap region (k≤18) shows no signal. **Do not chase this bridge; bank it as a separation (option 6).**

